order: Order of evaluation

Go evaluates the calls in an expression from left to right; C++ leaves the order of function
arguments and of most operands unspecified, and g++ and clang++ pick different orders. So
wherever the order could be seen (two calls, a call and a variable that the call can change,
two operations that can panic), the emitter evaluates the operands into gp_ temporaries
first, in Go's order: auto gp_t0 = f(); auto gp_t1 = g(); then the expression uses them.

A boxed variable, a package variable and *p can change in any call, so they are read after
the calls of their statement, as gc does: in x + f(), f runs first. An expression with at
most one call, or with operands that nothing can change, needs no temporaries and is written
as it is.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#order-of-evaluation
See also: gocpp explain boxing, gocpp explain panics
Also known as: evaluation-order, temporaries, temps

Example (order.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    var trace []string

    func f(name string, v int) int {
    	trace = append(trace, name)
    	return v
    }

    func main() {
    	x := f("a", 1) + f("b", 2)*f("c", 3)
    	m := map[string]int{"k": f("d", 4)}
    	fmt.Println(x, m, trace)
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    extern const gp::type_desc* const gp_tdc_0;
    extern const gp::type_desc* const gp_tdc_1;
    int64_t f(const gp::string& name, int64_t v);
    void main();

    extern gp::slice<gp::string> trace;

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/order.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/order.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./order.gp:7: func f(name string, v int) int {
    // gp[refcount] col 8: name is a const reference: the body never assigns it (a local that no call
    //     can change, or a temporary, is passed as it is: no copy, no count; anything else is copied
    //     first)
    int64_t f(const gp::string& name, int64_t v) {

    // ---- ./order.gp:8: trace = append(trace, name)
    // gp[allocation] col 10: append: writes in place when the capacity is enough, else allocates a
    //     bigger array (the capacity is Go's: its growth formula, element sizes and malloc size
    //     classes); the result may share the array with the argument
        trace = gp::append(static_cast<gp::slice<gp::string>&&>(trace), name);

    // ---- ./order.gp:9: return v
        return v;

    // ---- ./order.gp:10: }
    }

    // ---- ./order.gp:12: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./order.gp:13: x := f("a", 1) + f("b", 2)*f("c", 3)
    // gp[order] col 7: gp_t0 holds the result of a call (or a value that a later call could change): Go
    //     evaluates it first, in order, where C++ leaves the order of the operands unspecified
    // gp[refcount] col 9: lent to f without a reference count: a temporary, or a variable that no call
    //     can change, keeps the object alive (gp::lend)
    // gp[order] col 19: gp_t1 holds the result of a call (or a value that a later call could change):
    //     Go evaluates it first, in order, where C++ leaves the order of the operands unspecified
    // gp[refcount] col 21: lent to f without a reference count: a temporary, or a variable that no call
    //     can change, keeps the object alive (gp::lend)
    // gp[refcount] col 31: lent to f without a reference count: a temporary, or a variable that no call
    //     can change, keeps the object alive (gp::lend)
        auto gp_t0 = gpkg_main::f("a"_gs, 1); auto gp_t1 = gpkg_main::f("b"_gs, 2); int64_t x = gp_t0 + gp_t1 * gpkg_main::f("c"_gs, 3);

    // ---- ./order.gp:14: m := map[string]int{"k": f("d", 4)}
    // gp[maps] col 21: map literal: gp::map_of builds the map from the entries in gc's order (the
    //     constant keys first)
    // gp[refcount] col 29: lent to f without a reference count: a temporary, or a variable that no call
    //     can change, keeps the object alive (gp::lend)
        gp::map<gp::string, int64_t> m = gp::map_of<gp::string, int64_t>({{"k"_gs, gpkg_main::f("d"_gs, 4)}});

    // ---- ./order.gp:15: fmt.Println(x, m, trace)
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
        gp::fmt::Println(x, gp::fmt::as(gp_tdc_0, m), gp::fmt::as(gp_tdc_1, trace));

    // ---- ./order.gp:16: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg_desc.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg_desc.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    const gp::type_desc* const gp_tdc_0 = gp::td_map<gp::string, int64_t>(&gp::td_string, &gp::td_int);
    const gp::type_desc* const gp_tdc_1 = gp::td_slice<gp::string>(&gp::td_string);

    } // namespace gpkg_main
